# Tutorial 07 — Variables and Conditions
# [set], [inc], [dec], [if]/[elif]/[else]/[endif] — making the script remember and react.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_07_variables
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[set demo_var 0]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: I just set a variable called {b}demo_var{/b} to 0. You didn't see it happen — it's invisible to the player.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Variables let the script remember things. Conditions let it react. That's this entire lesson.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> [set] — creating and assigning variables -> section_set
> [inc] and [dec] — counting up and down -> section_math
> [if] / [elif] / [else] / [endif] — conditional blocks -> section_conditions
> Wiring variables to choices -> section_choices_vars
> Quick reference: state and branching -> quick_reference
> Practice: a scored quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Variables are the memory of your story:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set trust 0]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc trust]{/color} and {color=#8888cc}[dec trust]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if trust >= 2]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I trust you.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[else]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Not yet.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: initialize variables, keep names specific, and close every [if] with [endif].
> Start the lesson sections -> section_set
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [set] ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_set
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[set name value]{/b} creates a variable or overwrites an existing one.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set courage 0]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set player_name "Alex"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set chapter 1]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Values can be {b}numbers{/b} (0, 42, -1) or {b}strings{/b} ("Alex", "route_a").
Lavender: Variables are {b}global{/b} — once set, any script file in the project can read or change them.
Lavender: They persist for the entire playthrough and are included in save files.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: You can also set a variable to the result of math:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set total courage + kindness]{/color}
> Next: inc and dec -> section_math
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [inc] and [dec] ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_math
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[inc name]{/b} adds 1. {b}[dec name]{/b} subtracts 1.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set courage 0]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc courage]{/color}    — courage is now 1
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc courage]{/color}    — courage is now 2
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[dec courage]{/color}    — courage is now 1
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The typical pattern: the player makes a choice, you silently [inc] a variable. Later, you check how high it got.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Help the stranger -> helped{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label helped{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc kindness]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The player never sees the variable. They just see a story that remembers what they did.
> Next: conditions -> section_conditions
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [if] / [elif] / [else] / [endif] ───────────────────────────

@label section_conditions
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[if]{/b} starts a conditional block. The engine checks the condition and runs the matching branch.
narrator: Complete syntax:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if courage >= 3]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You've been incredibly brave.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[elif courage >= 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You showed some courage.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[else]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Maybe next time.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[if]{/b} — required. Checks the first condition.
Lavender: {b}[elif]{/b} — optional. Checks another condition if the previous was false. You can chain multiple.
Lavender: {b}[else]{/b} — optional. Catches everything that didn't match.
Lavender: {b}[endif]{/b} — {b}required{/b}. Every [if] must have exactly one [endif].
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Available operators:
narrator: {b}=={/b} equal   {b}!={/b} not equal   {b}>{/b} greater   {b}<{/b} less   {b}>={/b} greater or equal   {b}<={/b} less or equal
Lavender: String comparison works too:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if player_name == "Alex"]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep conditional blocks short. If you have more than 4 [elif]s, consider restructuring with labels and [jump].
> Next: wiring to choices -> section_choices_vars
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Variables + Choices ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_choices_vars
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The real payoff: combining variables with player choices. Here's a complete pattern:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: A kitten is stuck in the tree.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Climb up and rescue it -> choice_rescue{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Walk past -> choice_ignore{/color}
narrator: Then at each target label:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label choice_rescue{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc courage]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc kindness]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: The kitten purrs in your arms.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_choice]{/color}
narrator: And later:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label after_choice{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if kindness >= 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: You know, you're a good person.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: This is how affection systems, route unlocks, and branching endings work. Small [inc]s add up to big consequences.
> Practice: a scored quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[set quiz_score 0]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let's test the variable system live. I'll ask three questions. Your answers change a variable. At the end, the script reacts.
> Ready -> practice_q1

@label practice_q1
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 1: A stranger drops their wallet. What do you do?
> Pick it up and return it -> pq1_return
> Keep walking -> pq1_walk

@label pq1_return
[inc quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Kind. That's {b}[inc quiz_score]{/b} — your score is now 1.
[jump practice_q2]

@label pq1_walk
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Practical. No [inc] — score stays at 0.
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q2
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 2: There's a shortcut through a dark alley. The safe route takes longer.
> Take the alley -> pq2_alley
> Go the safe way -> pq2_safe

@label pq2_alley
[inc quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Brave. {b}[inc quiz_score]{/b} again.
[jump practice_q3]

@label pq2_safe
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Cautious. Score unchanged.
[jump practice_q3]

@label practice_q3
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 3: A friend asks for help moving on a rainy Saturday.
> Show up with coffee -> pq3_help
> Send a polite excuse -> pq3_skip

@label pq3_help
[inc quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Dependable. {b}[inc quiz_score]{/b}.
[jump practice_result]

@label pq3_skip
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Self-preserving. Score holds.
[jump practice_result]

@label practice_result
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: And now the conditional payoff:
[if quiz_score >= 3]
Lavender: Score: 3. You're the kind of person stories are written about.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if quiz_score >= 3]{/color} — this branch ran because all three [inc]s fired.
[elif quiz_score == 2]
Lavender: Score: 2. Brave and kind, with a practical streak.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[elif quiz_score == 2]{/color} — two out of three.
[elif quiz_score == 1]
Lavender: Score: 1. Selective generosity. One moment of courage stood out.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[elif quiz_score == 1]{/color} — only one [inc] fired.
[else]
Lavender: Score: 0. You play it safe. Some stories reward that.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[else]{/color} — no [inc]s fired, so this fallback ran.
[endif]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Three [set]/[inc] commands and one [if] block — that's the entire state system in action.
> Retake the quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_07]
[flag tutorial_done_07]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
